	    return __s1;
```
https://github.com/gcc-mirror/gcc/blob/master/libstdc%2B%2B-v3/include/bits/char_traits.h#L216-L217

issue_comment:
Also reproducible using:

```c++
constexpr const char *f (const char* a, const char *b) {
  if (a == b)
    return a;

  return b;
}


constexpr const char *k = f("a", "b");
```

If this:

```c++
constexpr auto foo(const char *p) { return p; }
constexpr auto p1 = "test1";
constexpr auto p2 = "test2";

constexpr bool b1 = foo(p1) == foo(p1);
static_assert(b1);

constexpr bool b2 = foo(p1) == foo(p2);
static_assert(!b2);

constexpr auto name1() { return "name1"; }
constexpr auto name2() { return "name2"; }

constexpr auto b3 = name1() == name1();
static_assert(b3);
constexpr auto b4 = name1() == name2();
static_assert(!b4);
```

should work without errors, is this a special case for character literals or should this work for every kind of literal? I can't come up with a test case for this right now with something that's not a string literal. But maybe it's just too early.

Is allowing this comparison really correct? 

If we create two globals with same string content, and ask about their address-equality at compile time, we now say that they're non-equal. Yet, at runtime, their addresses are in fact equal.

```
constexpr auto p1 = "test1";
constexpr auto p2 = "test1";
static_assert(p1 != p2);

int main() {
    const char *a = p1, *b = p2;
    return a == b;
}
```

The new behavior does not appear to be correct, and will lead to miscompiles in some circumstances. For example:

```c++
constexpr auto p1 = "test";
constexpr auto p2 = "test";
void g(), h();
void f() {
  const bool eq1 = p1 == p2;
  if (eq1) g();
  bool eq2 = p1 == p2;
  if (eq2) h();
}
```

... is required to call either both `g()` and `h()` or neither. After this change, clang trunk calls just `h()`, which is a miscompile.

It seems to me that treating the comparisons as non-constant is the least-bad thing we can do until CWG2765 is resolved, so I think we should revert the change.

