module : private;

unsigned a() {
	unsigned x = 0;
	REPEAT8(REPEAT8(REPEAT8(REPEAT8(REPEAT8(REPEAT8(++x))))));
	return x;
}
```

And then all that importers of `module a` would get is the function signature, thereby preventing the optimizer from even being able to run on the contents of `a()`. Does this expectation line up with the intended implementation in clang?

That being said, all of this discussion about private module fragments and noinlne is, in my mind, a workaround for the underlying problem that I outline in the previous parts of this bug. My ideal is that all of my functions are visible to the optimizer (either by adding `inline` to a bunch of functions in line with https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2019/p1498r1.html or by passing some optimization flag saying "inline across module boundaries by default"). However, I would want the work of optimization to be done once per function, when compiling the module that defines the function. Right now, the optimizer runs on the functions defined in modules I imported, even though the optimizer has already optimized those functions. The optimizer might run again because of inlining or other IPA passes, and that's probably fine, but I don't want to see the current O(n^2) scaling.

FWIW, I deal with expensive headers by creating shim modules.
Importing a shim module is faster than preprocessing the `#include`s.
So whenever I change my root module that triggers recompilation of all my modules,
the shim modules don't need to be recompiled
(and thus the `#include`s don't need to be preprocessed again).
Might you be able to do the same by relinquishing your expensive functions back to headers?

